= i
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

<Neutrino decoupling> occurs when the interaction time ceases to be short compared with the expansion time, or $\Gamma\simeq H$. In <natural units>, this gives
$$
G_\nu^2T_{\rm dec}^5=1.66\frac{\sqrt{g_*}\,T_{\rm dec}^2}{M_{\rm Pl}},\qquad T_{\rm dec}=\left(\frac{1.66\sqrt{g_*}}{G_\nu^2M_{\rm Pl}}\right)^{1/3}.
$$
Using the supplied high-temperature estimate $g_*\simeq100$,
$$
\boxed{T_{\rm dec}\simeq\left(\frac{16.6}{10^{-24}\times1.2\times10^{19}}\right)^{1/3}\!\mathrm{GeV}\simeq1.1\times10^2\,\mathrm{GeV}.}
$$
Here $M_{\rm Pl}$ is the unreduced <Planck mass>. The scaling $\Gamma/H\propto T^3$ explains the direction of the transition: interactions maintain <thermal equilibrium> at high temperature, but become ineffective as the universe cools. The extra species changes $g_*$ only by a small amount compared with the precision of the estimate.

= ii
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

A collisionless relativistic species redshifts with $T_\nu\propto a^{-1}$. The coupled plasma instead obeys <cosmological entropy conservation>, $g_{*s}T^3a^3=\mathrm{constant}$, and is heated relative to a decoupled species as massive particles annihilate. The <effective number of relativistic energy degrees of freedom> $g_*$ and <effective number of relativistic entropy degrees of freedom> $g_{*s}$ coincide while all species share a common relativistic temperature. Use $g_d:=g_{*s}(T_{\rm dec})\simeq100$ for the <Standard Model> bath, excluding the already decoupled extra species.

Just before ordinary <neutrino decoupling>, the coupled bath contains two <photon> states, four <electron>/<positron> states and six ordinary <neutrino>/<antineutrino> states. Therefore
$$
g_{*s}=2+\frac78(4+6)=\frac{43}{4}.
$$
The extra-neutrino temperature relative to that bath is then $(43/(4g_d))^{1/3}$. After the ordinary <neutrinos> decouple, <electron>/<positron> annihilation heats only the <photon> bath: its entropy count changes from $2+(7/8)4=11/2$ to $2$. Both <neutrino> populations miss that last heating, so
$$
\boxed{\frac{T_{\nu,0}}{T_0}=\left(\frac{43}{4g_d}\right)^{1/3}\left(\frac4{11}\right)^{1/3}=\left(\frac{43}{11g_d}\right)^{1/3}\simeq0.34.}
$$
This is the <two-stage entropy dilution of an early-decoupled neutrino>. Numerically **$T_{\nu,0}\simeq6.8\times10^{-14}\,\mathrm{GeV}$**. Relative to ordinary relic <neutrinos> it is cooler by $(43/(4g_d))^{1/3}\simeq0.48$.

Let $g_\nu$ count the extra <neutrino>'s populated helicity and antiparticle states. For one conventional <neutrino> plus <antineutrino> species $g_\nu=2$, the same state count as <photons>. The <Fermi-Dirac distribution> supplies the $3/4$ number-density factor, so
$$
\boxed{\frac{n_{\nu,0}}{n_{\gamma,0}}=\frac34\frac{g_\nu}{2}\left(\frac{T_{\nu,0}}{T_0}\right)^3=\frac{3g_\nu}{8}\frac{43}{11g_d}\simeq0.029\frac{g_\nu}{2}.}
$$
Thus a two-state species has about **$2.9\%$** of the <photon> number density. Stating $g_\nu$ explicitly also covers a different choice of populated states.

= 2
{parent=2}
{scope}

= i
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Since the species decouples while relativistic, its comoving particle number is conserved. Once it becomes nonrelativistic, its energy density is $\rho_{\nu,0}\simeq m_\nu n_{\nu,0}$. With $R_n:=n_{\nu,0}/n_{\gamma,0}$,
$$
\frac{\Omega_\nu}{\Omega_\gamma}\simeq\frac{m_\nu R_n}{\langle E_\gamma\rangle},\qquad m_\nu\lesssim\frac{0.1}{\Omega_\gamma}\frac{\langle E_\gamma\rangle}{R_n}.
$$
This is a <density bound on a relativistically decoupled massive relic>. Using literally the printed energy-density coefficient, the supplied number-density coefficient gives $\langle E_\gamma\rangle=(3.3/0.12)T_0=27.5T_0$. For $g_\nu=2$ and $g_d=100$,
$$
\boxed{m_\nu\lesssim2000\,\frac{27.5\times2\times10^{-13}}{0.0293}\,\mathrm{GeV}\simeq3.8\times10^{-7}\,\mathrm{GeV}\simeq4\times10^2\,\mathrm{eV}\quad\text{(printed normalization)}.}
$$
The estimate scales as $(2/g_\nu)(g_d/100)$. At the bound the particles are safely nonrelativistic today, so neglecting their kinetic energy is consistent.

There is a physical normalization defect in the printed coefficient. Direct integration of the <Bose-Einstein distribution> gives
$$
\frac{\rho}{g}=\frac{T^4}{2\pi^2}\int_0^\infty\frac{x^3\,dx}{e^x-1}=\frac{\pi^2}{30}T^4\simeq0.329T^4,
$$
which is also the coefficient consistent with the supplied $1.66$ expansion-rate formula. It implies $\langle E_\gamma\rangle=\pi^4T_0/(30\zeta(3))\simeq2.70T_0$, rather than $27.5T_0$. With this physically normalized <photon> gas, the same <density bound on a relativistically decoupled massive relic> becomes **$m_\nu\lesssim3.7\times10^1\,\mathrm{eV}$**, or about **$4\times10^1\,\mathrm{eV}$** using the rounded coefficients with $0.33$ in place of $3.3$. The two estimates differ by the printed factor of ten; the entropy-dilution result is unaffected.

= ii
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The preceding bound applies to a species that freezes out while relativistic, retaining an unsuppressed thermal number density apart from entropy dilution. It need not apply to a species that is already massive while reactions remain rapid.

For $m_\nu\gg T_{\rm dec}$, the interaction strength is sufficient to keep the species in <thermal equilibrium> through its relativistic-to-nonrelativistic transition. At $T\sim m_\nu$, the relativistic scaling would give $\Gamma/H\sim(m_\nu/T_{\rm dec})^3\gg1$. Subsequent annihilation removes most particle-antiparticle pairs while equilibrium remains possible. At negligible <chemical potential>, the nonrelativistic equilibrium number density is
$$
n_{\rm eq}\simeq g_\nu\left(\frac{m_\nu T}{2\pi}\right)^{3/2}e^{-m_\nu/T}.
$$
This <Boltzmann suppression of a nonrelativistic relic> can make the eventual abundance much smaller than the relativistically decoupled value. Freeze-out now occurs when $n_{\rm eq}\langle\sigma v\rangle\sim H$, with a nonrelativistic cross-section, rather than by extrapolating the massless $T^5$ rate indefinitely. A large mass can therefore have an acceptably small energy density: **a hot-relic mass bound is not a universal bound on a heavy thermal relic**.

Compatibility depends on the annihilation rate and any conserved particle-antiparticle asymmetry; the supplied relativistic rate alone does not determine the heavy relic's final abundance. Efficient annihilation is the mechanism that permits large masses within the standard expansion and thermal history.

= 3
{parent=Paper 53}
{scope}

= i
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

A light <inflaton> behaves approximately as a scalar field in de Sitter space while a wavelength is well inside the <Hubble radius>. Its <Bunch-Davies vacuum> has <quantum fluctuations>. <Cosmic inflation> stretches each <Fourier mode> until $k=aH$, after which its physical wavelength exceeds the <Hubble radius>. The nearly constant growing field mode has a typical fluctuation per logarithmic wavenumber interval $\delta\phi\sim H/(2\pi)$.

A field fluctuation changes the local position on the rolling background trajectory. Neighboring regions therefore reach the same field value, and the end of inflation, at slightly different times. A clock displacement of magnitude $|\delta\phi/\dot\phi|$ becomes a difference in local expansion of order $H|\delta\phi/\dot\phi|$. Equivalently, in a conventional sign choice the <comoving curvature perturbation> is related to the field fluctuation on spatially flat slices by
$$
\boxed{\mathcal R=\frac{H}{\dot\phi}\,\delta\phi_{\rm flat},\qquad |\mathcal R|\sim\frac{H}{|\dot\phi|}\frac{H}{2\pi}.}
$$
Changing the sign convention for spatial curvature changes the sign of $\mathcal R$, but not its spectrum. This is the <inflaton clock-shift origin of curvature perturbations>. For a single-field slow-roll attractor there is no independent entropy mode. On a <super-Hubble scale>, gradient terms are negligible and the <superhorizon conservation of single-field comoving curvature> preserves the growing <adiabatic mode>, so fluctuations generated near exit persist as primordial curvature perturbations. This conservation requires the attractor and adiabatic assumptions; a freely chosen non-attractor background would not have the same conclusion.

= ii
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The <slow-roll approximation> neglects $\ddot\phi$ relative to $3H\dot\phi$ and kinetic energy relative to the potential. It is self-consistent on the attractor when the <potential slow-roll parameter> $\epsilon\ll1$ and the <second potential slow-roll parameter> $|\eta|\ll1$. Thus
$$
3H\dot\phi\simeq-V',\qquad H^2\simeq\frac{V}{3M_{\rm pl}^2},\qquad \epsilon=\frac{M_{\rm pl}^2}{2}\left(\frac{V'}V\right)^2,\qquad \eta=M_{\rm pl}^2\frac{V''}V.
$$
Here, as in the <inflaton> equations, $M_{\rm pl}$ denotes the <reduced Planck mass>, not the unreduced mass used in the thermal calculation. The <slow-roll curvature power spectrum> becomes
$$
\Delta_{\mathcal R}^2=\frac{H^4}{4\pi^2\dot\phi^2}\simeq\frac{9H^6}{4\pi^2V'^2}=\frac{V^3}{12\pi^2M_{\rm pl}^6V'^2},
$$
so
$$
\boxed{\Delta_{\mathcal R}^2(k)\simeq\left.\frac{V}{24\pi^2M_{\rm pl}^4\epsilon}\right|_{k=aH}.}
$$

To differentiate with respect to horizon-exit scale, write $\mathcal N=\ln a$, increasing with physical time. Along the slow-roll trajectory,
$$
\frac{d\phi}{d\mathcal N}=\frac{\dot\phi}{H}\simeq-M_{\rm pl}^2\frac{V'}V,\qquad\frac{d\ln k}{d\mathcal N}=1-\epsilon_H\simeq1-\epsilon.
$$
The difference between differentiating with respect to $\ln k$ and $\mathcal N$ contributes only at second slow-roll order to the tilt. Since $\ln\Delta_{\mathcal R}^2=3\ln V-2\ln|V'|+\mathrm{constant}$,
$$
\begin{aligned}
n_s-1&\simeq-M_{\rm pl}^2\frac{V'}V\left(3\frac{V'}V-2\frac{V''}{V'}\right)\\
&=-3M_{\rm pl}^2\left(\frac{V'}V\right)^2+2M_{\rm pl}^2\frac{V''}V.
\end{aligned}
$$
Therefore the <scalar spectral index in potential slow-roll parameters> is
$$
\boxed{n_s\simeq1-6\epsilon+2\eta.}
$$
All background quantities in these expressions are evaluated when the particular mode exits the <Hubble radius>.

= iii
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

For the <quadratic-potential slow-roll solution>, $V'/V=2/\phi$, so $\epsilon=\eta=2M_{\rm pl}^2/\phi^2$. Inflation ends when $\epsilon\simeq1$, giving $\phi_{\rm end}^2=2M_{\rm pl}^2$. The <slow-roll e-fold count> remaining before that endpoint is
$$
N_*\simeq\frac1{M_{\rm pl}^2}\int_{\phi_{\rm end}}^{\phi_*}\frac{V}{V'}\,d\phi=\frac{\phi_*^2-\phi_{\rm end}^2}{4M_{\rm pl}^2}.
$$
With $N_*=50$, this gives $\phi_*^2=202M_{\rm pl}^2$ and $\epsilon_*=\eta_*=1/101$. The <quadratic-inflation amplitude calibration> is
$$
\Delta_{\mathcal R}^2(k_*)=\frac{m^2\phi_*^4}{96\pi^2M_{\rm pl}^6}=\frac{m^2}{M_{\rm pl}^2}\frac{(2N_*+1)^2}{24\pi^2}.
$$
Matching the supplied amplitude therefore gives
$$
\boxed{\frac{m}{M_{\rm pl}}=\frac{\sqrt{24\pi^2(2.2\times10^{-9})}}{101}\simeq7.15\times10^{-6}.}
$$
Taking $M_{\rm pl}\simeq2.4\times10^{18}\,\mathrm{GeV}$, this is **$m\simeq1.7\times10^{13}\,\mathrm{GeV}$**. Retaining $\phi_{\rm end}$ produces the small $+1$ correction; dropping it gives almost the same leading slow-roll estimate.

The <scalar spectral index> is
$$
\boxed{n_s(k_*)\simeq1-\frac4{101}\simeq0.9604.}
$$
It agrees with the supplied central value and lies well inside the stated uncertainty interval. This conclusion concerns the scalar tilt; the tensor prediction is a separate test of the same model.

= iv
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}

Divide the <primordial tensor power spectrum> by the <slow-roll curvature power spectrum>:
$$
r=\frac{8H^2/(4\pi^2M_{\rm pl}^2)}{H^4/(4\pi^2\dot\phi^2)}=\frac{8\dot\phi^2}{M_{\rm pl}^2H^2}.
$$
Differentiating the scalar-field <Friedmann equation> and using the <inflaton> equation gives $\dot H=-\dot\phi^2/(2M_{\rm pl}^2)$. Hence $r=16\epsilon_H\simeq16\epsilon$. For the quadratic model at 50 remaining e-folds,
$$
\boxed{r\simeq\frac{16}{101}\simeq0.158.}
$$
This is above the supplied upper bound, **so the model fails the stated tensor constraint**, despite its satisfactory scalar tilt and adjustable scalar amplitude. Calibrating $m$ does not reduce $r$, since the coupling cancels from the ratio.

= 4
{parent=Paper 53}
{scope}

= i
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

An <adiabatic mode> corresponds locally to a common displacement along the background history. Its density changes obey $\delta\rho_i/\dot{\bar\rho}_i=\delta t$ for every component. The <cosmological perfect-fluid continuity equation> then implies that $\Delta_i/(1+w_i)$ is common to all fluids. Thus, in the common <comoving gauge in cosmology> relevant to the growing superhorizon mode,
$$
\boxed{\Delta_r=\frac43\Delta_c.}
$$
Equivalently, the relative entropy perturbation $\Delta_c-(3/4)\Delta_r$ vanishes. A common time-slicing shift preserves this relation because it changes both normalized density perturbations by the same amount.

The flat background <Friedmann equation> gives $4\pi Ga^2\bar\rho=(3/2)\mathcal H^2$. During <radiation domination>, $\mathcal H=1/\tau$ and the matter background is negligible. Substituting the adiabatic relation into the density equation therefore gives
$$
\Delta_c''+\frac1\tau\Delta_c'=\frac4{\tau^2}\Delta_c.
$$
With $\Delta_c\propto\tau^p$, the indicial equation is $p^2=4$, so the modes are $\tau^2$ and $\tau^{-2}$.

During <matter domination>, $\mathcal H=2/\tau$ and the radiation background is negligible. The same calculation gives
$$
\Delta_c''+\frac2\tau\Delta_c'=\frac6{\tau^2}\Delta_c,
$$
whose indicial equation is $p^2+p-6=(p-2)(p+3)=0$. Its modes are $\tau^2$ and $\tau^{-3}$. Thus the <superhorizon growing density mode of adiabatic matter and radiation> satisfies **$\Delta_c\propto\tau^2$ in both eras**. The common conformal-time exponent does not imply the same scale-factor dependence: it is $\Delta_c\propto a^2$ in the radiation era and $\Delta_c\propto a$ in the matter era.

= ii
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Once a mode is well inside the horizon, radiation pressure opposes gravitational collapse. For an ideal radiation fluid its <cosmological sound speed> is $c_s=1/\sqrt3$, and its density perturbation undergoes <subhorizon radiation acoustic oscillations> with frequency approximately $k/\sqrt3$. Unlike the superhorizon growing mode, its amplitude does not acquire sustained gravitational growth. Free-streaming relativistic components can additionally phase-mix the perturbation; the acoustic-fluid description is the appropriate idealization for the stated system.

During deep <radiation domination>, the radiation source in the dark-matter equation oscillates rapidly compared with a Hubble time. Its accumulated late-time effect averages away. More quantitatively, an oscillatory source of magnitude $\Delta_r/\tau^2$ gives a rapidly varying particular solution of order $\Delta_r/(k\tau)^2$ when $k\tau\gg1$. It can therefore be neglected in computing the slowly varying homogeneous dark-matter growth, although its forcing near horizon entry fixes the integration constants.

The dark-matter self-gravity term is also subleading while $a/a_{\rm eq}\ll1$. Keeping the leading expansion drag and averaging the radiation source yields
$$
\Delta_c''+\frac1\tau\Delta_c'\simeq0,\qquad (\tau\Delta_c')'\simeq0.
$$
Consequently
$$
\boxed{\Delta_c=C_1+C_2\ln(\tau/\tau_h),\qquad \Delta_{c,\rm grow}\propto\ln\tau.}
$$
This <logarithmic growth of matter perturbations during radiation domination> is the <Mészáros effect>. It is the leading behavior well after horizon entry but before equality; close to equality the neglected self-gravity matters. The radiation perturbation is not small simply because it is radiation: its rapid oscillations, rather than a small radiation background density, justify dropping its source for this slow mode.

= iii
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

In <matter domination>, the radiation source is negligible and cold matter has negligible pressure. The comoving density equation consequently contains no Jeans-pressure term proportional to $k^2\Delta_c$. On every linear scale, including superhorizon and subhorizon modes, it reduces to
$$
\Delta_c''+\frac2\tau\Delta_c'-\frac6{\tau^2}\Delta_c=0.
$$
The <matter-era growing and decaying density modes> are therefore
$$
\boxed{\Delta_c=C_g\tau^2+C_d\tau^{-3},\qquad\Delta_{c,\rm grow}\propto\tau^2\propto a.}
$$
The absence of a $k$-dependent pressure term makes the time dependence scale-independent; the coefficients still depend on wavenumber and on the earlier radiation-era evolution. This is linear growth in the stated cold-matter model, before nonlinear collapse or any additional late-time component changes the evolution.

= iv
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}

Write $\mathscr P_c(k,\tau)=k^3|\Delta_c(k,\tau)|^2/(2\pi^2)$ for the <dimensionless power spectrum>. A radiation-era mode enters the horizon at $\tau_h\sim k^{-1}$. The early superhorizon law gives $\mathscr P_c(k,\tau_h)\sim A$: the $\tau_h^4$ and $k^4$ factors cancel.

For $k\tau_{\rm eq}\ll1$, the mode remains outside the horizon throughout the radiation era. Its amplitude grows as $\tau^2$ both before and after equality, and continues with the same scale-independent growing factor even if it later enters during <matter domination>. Hence the low-wavenumber shape is preserved:
$$
\boxed{\mathscr P_c(k,\tau)\sim A\tau^4k^4,\qquad k\tau_{\rm eq}\ll1.}
$$
For $k\tau_{\rm eq}\gg1$, horizon entry occurs during <radiation domination>. Its subsequent logarithmic amplitude growth supplies a factor $\ln(\tau_{\rm eq}/\tau_h)\sim\ln(k\tau_{\rm eq})$ by equality. During the matter era the amplitude grows by $(\tau/\tau_{\rm eq})^2$. Squaring gives the <logarithmic high-wavenumber density-spectrum transfer>:
$$
\boxed{\mathscr P_c(k,\tau)\sim A\left(\frac{\tau}{\tau_{\rm eq}}\right)^4[\ln(k\tau_{\rm eq})]^2,\qquad k\tau_{\rm eq}\gg1.}
$$
The horizon-entry and equality matching fixes order-one coefficients and additive constants inside the logarithm. The displayed branches capture the requested leading spectrum and share its schematic normalization; their asymptotic forms must not be joined literally at $k\tau_{\rm eq}=1$, where the high-$k$ logarithm alone vanishes.

Thus the present <dimensionless power spectrum> rises as $k^4$ on large scales and only as $\ln^2 k$ on small scales, with a smooth bend near the <matter-radiation equality scale>, $k_{\rm eq}\sim\tau_{\rm eq}^{-1}$. The ordinary dimensional <cosmological density power spectrum> instead satisfies
$$
P_c(k)=\frac{2\pi^2}{k^3}\mathscr P_c(k)\propto\begin{cases}k,&k\ll k_{\rm eq},\\ k^{-3}\ln^2(k/k_{\rm eq}),&k\gg k_{\rm eq}.
\end{cases}
$$
It has a turnover near equality; the dimensionless spectrum does not. The following comparison of <dimensional and dimensionless matter spectra across equality> uses a smooth guide with the correct asymptotes, not an exact solution through the transition. Overall vertical normalization and the later scale-independent growth factor are suppressed.

\Image[paper-53-matter-spectrum.png]
{title=Present matter spectrum across equality: dimensionless power bends from k to the fourth power to logarithmic growth, while dimensional power turns over}
